<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="epoxysqualene biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5670" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: SQUALENE Squalene| is a naturally occurring polyprenyl compound primarily known for its key role as an intermediate in sterol biosynthesis. The name originates from the scientific name for the shark (|FRAME: TAX-7796| spp.), since shark liver oil is considered the richest source of squalene. However, squalene is abundant in many plant oils, including olive oil, palm oil, wheat-germ oil, amaranth oil, and rice bran oil. Squalene is structurally similar to |FRAME: CPD1F-129|, and is produced in two steps from two molecules of |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| which are joined and reduced by the enzyme |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.21|. After its biosynthesis, squalene can be transported to other areas of the body for incorporation into tissues (in humans, about 60 percent of dietary squalene is absorbed and transported in serum to be distributed ubiquitously in different tissues, with the greatest concentration in the skin |CITS: [9988781]|) or it can be further metabolized by the enzyme |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.17|, generating |FRAME: EPOXYSQUALENE|. The fate of |FRAME: EPOXYSQUALENE| varies in different organisms. In animals and fungi it is substrate for |FRAME: EC-5.4.99.7|, an enzyme that generates |FRAME: LANOSTEROL|, a main gateway for the synthesis of all other sterols in these organisms, including cholesterol (see |FRAME: PWY66-341|). In plants |FRAME: EPOXYSQUALENE| is processed by |FRAME: EC-5.4.99.8|, which generates |FRAME: CYCLOARTENOL|. This compound is then converted to many different sterol compounds (see |FRAME: PWY-2541|). Squalene and its derivatives appear to be very common in the Archaea. A study of the lipid composition of the thermophilic chemolithotroph |FRAME: TAX-145262| showed that squalene was the major component of the non-polar lipids |CITS: [691077]|. Another study of the neutral lipids of nine species of methanogens found that squalene and/or hydrosqualene derivatives were the predominant components in eight of them |CITS: [458874]|. Sterol biosynthesis by eubacteria is very rare. However, steroid precursors including |FRAME: LANOSTEROL| and |FRAME: CYCLOARTENOL| were isolated from several prokaryotes, including |FRAME: TAX-41| |CITS: [12519197]|, |FRAME: TAX-54| |CITS: [Kohl83]|, |FRAME: TAX-114| |CITS: [14660793]| and |FRAME: TAX-51353| |CITS: [10802170]|. Genes encoding the enzymes |FRAME: MONOMER-14485| and |FRAME: MONOMER-14484| have been cloned from |FRAME: TAX-414|, which produces several modified lanosterol products (see |FRAME: PWY-8026|) |CITS: [17928701]|.</Description>
